{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The historical rail line connecting Seattle to the rest of the West Coast corridor -- this is the Southern Pacific's 'Cascade', which ran Oakland-Portland with through sleeping cars to Seattle. Combined with the existing Coast Daylight (LA-San Francisco, see southern_pacific_coast_daylight.geojson), this is the direct historical predecessor of Amtrak's Coast Starlight: the two trains were merged in 1971-72 to create it. Geometry is real-traced from OpenStreetMap active-rail geometry for nearly the entire 1400km route (Oakland through Redding, Dunsmuir, Klamath Falls, Eugene, Portland, and up through Washington to Seattle) -- this whole corridor is still active track today, since it's the same route the modern Coast Starlight runs. One short ~28km gap (Suisun to Davis, CA, likely a swing-bridge/causeway tagging quirk in OSM) could not be matched to a connected path and is a straight-line approximation.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cascade_(train) (Southern Pacific's Cascade, Oakland-Portland with through sleepers to Seattle, ran April 17 1927 - April 30 1971; route via the Cascade Line/Natron Cutoff opened 1926; Portland-Seattle segment operated as a Union Pacific pool train, equipment transferred to Northern Pacific at Seattle; streamlined service with through Seattle sleepers began Aug 13 1950; Seattle sleeper service discontinued Jan 9 1966; Amtrak combined this with the Coast Daylight in 1971-72 to form the Coast Starlight)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast_Starlight (confirms the Coast Starlight's direct lineage from the Cascade + Coast Daylight)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for any era; precise station list beyond the major cities used for routing.", "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API), railway=rail ways tagged usage=main, for the full Oakland-Redding-Dunsmuir-Klamath Falls-Eugene-Portland-Seattle corridor"], "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Southern Pacific Railroad", "line": "Southern Pacific: Cascade", "id": "sp-cascade-oakland-portland-seattle", "color": "#C1272D", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 70, "speed_source": "estimated: SP mainline steam/diesel intercity service over the Cascade Line (Natron Cutoff), including mountain grades; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1927, "end": 1950, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Cascade launched April 17 1927 over the newly-opened (1926) Cascade Line/Natron Cutoff; heavyweight equipment era; estimated ~1 round trip/day, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cascade_(train", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast_Starlight"]}, {"start": 1950, "end": 1966, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Streamlined service inaugurated Aug 13 1950, with through sleeping cars Oakland-Seattle (Portland-Seattle operated as a Union Pacific pool train, equipment transferred to Northern Pacific at Seattle); estimated ~1 round trip/day, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cascade_(train", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast_Starlight"]}, {"start": 1966, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Seattle sleeping car service discontinued Jan 9 1966, though Portland-Seattle rail service continued via connecting trains; Cascade itself ran until Amtrak's May 1 1971 takeover, when it was combined with the Coast Daylight to form the Coast Starlight", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cascade_(train", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast_Starlight"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-123.1084, 44.32793], "to": [-123.1071, 44.62501], "chord_mi": 20.53, "traced_mi": 20.53, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Brooklyn Subdivision (20.53 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.93127, 45.08189], "to": [-122.76907, 45.21353], "chord_mi": 12.25, "traced_mi": 12.05, "snap_m": [1, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Brooklyn Subdivision (12.05 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.04151, 38.2434], "to": [-121.73853, 38.5463], "chord_mi": 25.94, "traced_mi": 26.95, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Martinez Subdivision (26.49 mi)", "West Valley Subdivision (0.46 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.1902, 39.22641], "to": [-122.19529, 39.76292], "chord_mi": 37.07, "traced_mi": 37.08, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["West Valley Subdivision (37.08 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"da